  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why does aluminium smelting need so much electricity?",
      "a": "Aluminium holds on to oxygen extremely tightly, so a lot of energy is needed to separate them. Smelters pass hundreds of thousands of amps through rows of cells day and night, which is why they are often built near hydroelectric dams.",
      "asks": [
        "How much electricity does making aluminium use?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do the carbon anodes get used up?",
      "a": "The oxygen released from the alumina reacts with the hot carbon anodes and forms carbon dioxide, so the anodes slowly burn away and must be replaced every few weeks.",
      "asks": [
        "What are the anodes made of in aluminium smelting?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is cryolite for?",
      "a": "Cryolite is a mineral of sodium, aluminium and fluorine. Molten cryolite dissolves alumina at about 960 degrees Celsius, so the cell does not need to reach the 2,000 plus degrees needed to melt alumina itself.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is alumina dissolved in cryolite?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does alumina come from?",
      "a": "Alumina, aluminium oxide, is extracted from a reddish ore called bauxite by dissolving it in hot caustic soda, filtering out the impurities and heating the crystals. About two tonnes of alumina make one tonne of aluminium.",
      "asks": [
        "What is bauxite?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is recycling aluminium so important?",
      "a": "Remelting aluminium uses only about 5 percent of the energy needed to make it from ore, and it can be recycled endlessly. A recycled can can be back on the shelf within a couple of months.",
      "asks": [
        "How much energy does recycling aluminium save?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The Hall–Héroult process was discovered independently in 1886 by Charles Martin Hall in the USA and Paul Héroult in France.",
    "Recycling aluminium uses about 5 percent of the energy needed to make it from ore."
  ],
